WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.557
Secure Go development starts with simple,
repeatable habits.

2
00:00:03.637 --> 00:00:06.579
Define trust boundaries before code starts
relying on data.

3
00:00:06.659 --> 00:00:08.389
Treat external input as untrusted.

4
00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:10.488
Parse and validate close to the boundary.

5
00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:14.721
Use types, small interfaces, and explicit
conversions to make assumptions visible.

6
00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:16.243
Handle errors deliberately.

7
00:00:16.323 --> 00:00:19.034
An ignored error can hide a failed check,
partial write,

8
00:00:19.114 --> 00:00:21.768
missing configuration value, or broken
cleanup path.

9
00:00:21.848 --> 00:00:25.886
Limit resources with bounded reads,
timeouts, cleanup, and lifecycle ownership.

10
00:00:25.966 --> 00:00:29.888
Protect secrets by keeping them out of
source, logs, metrics, client responses,

11
00:00:29.968 --> 00:00:31.525
and broad operational systems.

12
00:00:31.605 --> 00:00:33.105
Review concurrency with care.

13
00:00:33.185 --> 00:00:37.165
Goroutines need owners, contexts need
cancellation, channels need clear rules,

14
00:00:37.245 --> 00:00:39.264
and shared state needs synchronization.

15
00:00:39.344 --> 00:00:43.382
Review HTTP handlers and service
boundaries for authentication, authorization,

16
00:00:43.462 --> 00:00:45.711
safe responses, logging, and request
limits.

17
00:00:45.791 --> 00:00:49.252
Manage dependencies and builds as part of
the application trust model.

18
00:00:49.332 --> 00:00:52.043
A secure Go application is not secure
because it uses Go.

19
00:00:52.123 --> 00:00:55.296
It is secure because its design, code,
tests, delivery pipeline,

20
00:00:55.376 --> 00:00:58.260
and operations make safe behavior visible
and repeatable.

21
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.589
Go's clarity gives teams a strong
foundation.

22
00:01:00.669 --> 00:01:04.188
Disciplined engineering turns that
foundation into durable security.

23
00:01:04.268 --> 00:01:07.671
The habits are intentionally ordinary:
check inputs, handle errors,

24
00:01:07.751 --> 00:01:10.693
bound work, review dependencies, and make
ownership clear.

25
00:01:10.773 --> 00:01:14.580
Repeating those habits across services is
what keeps security maintainable.
